cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this tool or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Production Line Bottleneck Finder"
authors:
  - family-names: Golchin
    given-names: Omid
version: 2.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-14
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A browser tool that finds a serial production line's bottleneck three
  ways: by simulating the line period by period and scoring which
  station's capacity binds, by checking the result against Little's Law
  and Kingman's heavy-traffic approximation, and by reading a process
  log with the active-period method. It prices every single change
  (capacity or buffer, station by station) with paired simulation runs,
  and compares push, drum-buffer-rope and CONWIP release at matched
  output. The underlying discrete-period simulator, after the dice game
  in Goldratt and Cox's The Goal, also carries the studies in the
  research notes, including a check of whether large language models
  can find a bottleneck directly, by writing a simulation, and from a
  process log.
keywords:
  - theory of constraints
  - bottleneck detection
  - drum-buffer-rope
  - CONWIP
  - Little's Law
  - Kingman approximation
  - active period method
  - simulation
